[
  {
    "language_code": "",
    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "So let's follow one chip. It starts life in a factory, say in Texas or Malaysia or the Philippines. It's sold to a large legitimate distributor in Europe or Asia. A small trading company in Hong Kong orders five thousand of them and says they're for consumer electronics. Some sit in a warehouse for a few weeks. Then they're repacked, relabeled as phone parts, and loaded onto a cargo flight or a truck. They cross into Kazakhstan or straight into Russia where a Russian importer signs for them. A few weeks after that, one of those chips is soldered onto a circuit board inside a drone rolling off a Russian assembly line. Nobody along the way had to break into anything. Each step looked like an ordinary business deal.",
